# Application Kconfig for the tc_uart sample. Zephyr sources the
# application-root Kconfig automatically; `source "Kconfig.zephyr"`
# keeps every base symbol available so prj.conf still works.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"

config FSW_PUS3_HK_PERIOD_SEC
	int "PUS-3 housekeeping report period (seconds)"
	default 5
	range 1 86400
	help
	  Initial value of datapool parameter 0x0001 — the interval between
	  spontaneous PUS-3[25] housekeeping parameter reports emitted by
	  the main loop. The cadence is a coarse main-loop elapsed-time
	  check against the FSW clock (k_uptime_get()/1000), not a kernel
	  timer. As of slice fsw-9 the period is a live-tunable datapool
	  parameter: a PUS-20[3] set retunes the cadence with no rebuild,
	  and a value of 0 disables periodic housekeeping. This symbol only
	  seeds the initial value. The default (5 s) is operationally sane.
	  The Renode closed-loop suite builds two
	  artefacts from this one sample: the verification-stream ELF pins
	  this to 86400 (effectively "never within a test" — Renode
	  fast-forwards idle virtual time, so a fixed short period would
	  race the fixed-offset reads non-deterministically), and a
	  dedicated housekeeping ELF pins it to 2 s for fast, deterministic
	  cadence assertions.

config FSW_FDIR_EVENT_FIFO_CAPACITY
	int "FDIR event FIFO capacity (records)"
	default 16
	range 4 256
	help
	  Number of records the bounded FDIR event FIFO holds before it
	  starts dropping the newest (the causal head — the first faults —
	  is preserved). Wired into the freestanding lib via the
	  MIGRIS_FDIR_EVENT_FIFO_CAPACITY compile definition so flight and
	  host builds size the ring identically. The default (16) absorbs a
	  burst of rejected TCs plus RX-overflow events between main-loop
	  drains; raise it only if a producer can outpace one drain per
	  loop iteration.

config FSW_DATAPOOL_CAPACITY
	int "Parameter datapool capacity (parameters)"
	default 8
	range 1 256
	help
	  Number of parameters the on-board datapool can hold. Wired into
	  the freestanding lib via the MIGRIS_DATAPOOL_CAPACITY compile
	  definition so flight and host builds size the store identically.
	  This sample registers a single parameter (the housekeeping
	  period); the default (8) leaves room for a mission to add more.

config FSW_PUS20_MAX_PARAMS
	int "PUS-20 maximum parameters per TC"
	default 8
	range 1 8
	help
	  Upper bound on the parameters one PUS-20 TC may address and one
	  [20,2] report may carry. Wired into the freestanding lib via the
	  MIGRIS_PUS20_MAX_PARAMS_PER_TC compile definition; it bounds the
	  worst-case [20,2] packet. Capped at 8 here because the TC router
	  output buffer (MIGRIS_TC_ROUTER_MAX_TM, a fixed 128) is sized for
	  that worst case — a larger value needs that constant raised too.

config FSW_SCHEDULE_CAPACITY
	int "On-board schedule capacity (activities)"
	default 16
	range 1 256
	help
	  Number of time-tagged telecommands the on-board schedule can
	  hold. Wired into the freestanding lib via the
	  MIGRIS_SCHEDULE_CAPACITY compile definition so flight and host
	  builds size the store identically.

config FSW_SCHEDULE_TC_MAX
	int "On-board schedule per-activity TC size (bytes)"
	default 64
	range 16 256
	help
	  Largest telecommand, in bytes, that one scheduled activity can
	  hold. Wired into the freestanding lib via the
	  MIGRIS_SCHEDULE_TC_MAX compile definition. 64 covers the
	  PUS-17 / PUS-20-class leaf commands of the baseline; a PUS-11
	  insert carrying a larger telecommand is rejected.

config FSW_PUS11_MAX_PER_TC
	int "PUS-11 maximum activities per TC"
	default 8
	range 1 8
	help
	  Upper bound on the activities one PUS-11 insert / delete / report
	  TC may carry, and one [11,12] summary report may list. Wired into
	  the freestanding lib via the MIGRIS_PUS11_MAX_PER_TC compile
	  definition; it bounds the worst-case [11,12] packet. Capped at 8
	  here because the TC router output buffer (MIGRIS_TC_ROUTER_MAX_TM,
	  a fixed 128) is sized for that worst case.

config FSW_PKTSTORE_CAPACITY
	int "On-board packet store capacity (packets)"
	default 32
	range 4 256
	help
	  Number of telemetry packets the on-board packet store can hold
	  before it overwrites the oldest. Wired into the freestanding lib
	  via the MIGRIS_PKTSTORE_CAPACITY compile definition so flight and
	  host builds size the store identically. The store is RAM-backed
	  and volatile — it is empty after every reboot. Storage is
	  CAPACITY x (PACKET_MAX + a little overhead) bytes of BSS; the
	  default (32) is a few KB on the H7 and absorbs a pass's worth of
	  housekeeping plus verification telemetry.

config FSW_PKTSTORE_PACKET_MAX
	int "On-board packet store per-packet size (bytes)"
	default 128
	range 80 128
	help
	  Largest telemetry packet, in bytes, one packet-store entry can
	  hold. Wired into the freestanding lib via the
	  MIGRIS_PKTSTORE_PACKET_MAX compile definition. A larger packet is
	  refused by migris_pktstore_store and skipped by the transmit_tm
	  tap. Capped at 128 because the retrieval drain re-emits a stored
	  packet through the shared 128-byte router output buffer
	  (MIGRIS_TC_ROUTER_MAX_TM); the floor (80) stays clear of the
	  largest baseline TM packet (a PUS-20[2] value report).

config FSW_LARGEDATA_DEMO
	bool "Run a PUS-13 large-data downlink demo at boot"
	default n
	help
	  When set, the tc_uart sample starts one PUS-13 large-data
	  transfer at boot: a synthetic data unit is downlinked as an
	  ordered sequence of [13,1] / [13,2] / [13,3] part packets, one
	  part per main-loop iteration. PUS-13 (lib/pus/pus13.{h,c} +
	  lib/largedata/) has no inbound telecommand, so a closed-loop
	  test cannot trigger a transfer the way it triggers PUS-11 or
	  PUS-15 — the demo is the observable surface for the reassembly
	  closed-loop in tests/renode/test_tc_uart_hk.py. Left off by
	  default: the verification-stream ELF (test_tc_uart.py) reads a
	  fixed byte count per stimulus and must not see unsolicited
	  large-data telemetry. The Renode housekeeping build turns it on.

config FSW_MODE_DEMO
	bool "Run an operating-mode-manager demo at boot"
	default n
	help
	  When set, the tc_uart sample wires an operating-mode manager and
	  performs one demo transition at boot (BOOT -> NOMINAL); the mode
	  manager announces it as a spontaneous PUS-5 MODE_CHANGED event.
	  The mode manager (lib/mode/) has no inbound telecommand — a
	  ground mode-commanding service is downstream (cry4-fsw) — so the
	  demo is the observable surface for the closed-loop test in
	  tests/renode/test_tc_uart_hk.py. Left off by default for the
	  same reason as FSW_LARGEDATA_DEMO: the verification-stream ELF
	  reads a fixed byte count per stimulus. The housekeeping build
	  turns it on.

config FSW_MODE_CAPACITY
	int "Operating-mode manager capacity (modes)"
	default 8
	range 2 32
	help
	  Number of distinct operating modes the on-board mode manager can
	  hold. Wired into the freestanding lib via the MIGRIS_MODE_CAPACITY
	  compile definition so flight and host builds size the manager
	  identically. This sample defines three modes (boot, nominal,
	  safe); the default (8) leaves room for a mission to add more.
	  Capped at 32 — a mode ID must fit the allowed-target bitmask.
